00:02:12.946 --> 00:02:13.799
Part 1.

24
00:02:14.079 --> 00:02:15.615
The System in One Page.

25
00:02:16.865 --> 00:02:18.593
What a logistics network does.

26
00:02:18.873 --> 00:02:26.169
A shipper (an online store) wants a parcel to go from its warehouse in Bhiwandi to a customer in a small town in Assam.

27
00:02:26.449 --> 00:02:35.239
It wants: a promised delivery date;. tracking;. cash collected if the customer pays on delivery;. the parcel back if the customer refuses.

28
00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:41.961
The network makes that happen for millions of parcels a day by: consolidating them into bags, trucks and flights;.

29
00:02:43.311 --> 00:02:46.831
sorting them at hubs;. splitting them back out to delivery agents.

30
00:02:48.181 --> 00:02:49.269
Main systems.

31
00:02:49.549 --> 00:02:50.915
Here is a quick table:

32
00:02:51.195 --> 00:02:52.709
System, and What it does.

33
00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.269
Client integration:

34
00:02:54.549 --> 00:03:01.077
APIs and panels for shippers: create shipments, labels, pickups, tracking, returns, invoices.

35
00:03:01.357 --> 00:03:03.149
Serviceability and TAT:

36
00:03:03.429 --> 00:03:08.229
Which pincodes are served, which services (express, surface, COD), promised days.

37
00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:09.896
Address intelligence:

38
00:03:10.176 --> 00:03:14.997
Parse, validate and geocode Indian addresses; assign to delivery centres.

39
00:03:15.277 --> 00:03:16.643
Pickup and first mile:

40
