Scan events, performance analytics, ML.

61
00:04:23.055 --> 00:04:25.209
Scale for this design (assumptions).

62
00:04:25.489 --> 00:04:26.855
Here is a quick table:

63
00:04:27.135 --> 00:04:28.457
Quantity, and Value.

64
00:04:28.737 --> 00:04:29.932
Parcels per day:

65
00:04:30.212 --> 00:04:32.836
3M normal, 8M on sale peaks.

66
00:04:33.116 --> 00:04:36.273
Pincodes served: ~18,000 (VERIFY).

67
00:04:36.553 --> 00:04:44.361
Facilities: ~25 large sort hubs, ~300 intermediate hubs, ~3,000 delivery centres.

68
00:04:44.641 --> 00:04:48.844
Line-haul trips per day: ~20,000 truck trips (plus air).

69
00:04:49.124 --> 00:04:51.769
Delivery agents: ~60,000.

70
00:04:52.049 --> 00:04:59.004
Scan events per parcel: ~15 to 25 (pickup, hub in or out, bag, trip, delivery attempts).

71
00:04:59.284 --> 00:05:03.615
Scan events per day: ~60M normal, 200M at peak.

72
00:05:03.895 --> 00:05:08.844
Tracking queries per day: ~100M (customers, shippers' systems polling).

73
00:05:10.194 --> 00:05:11.581
Estimation highlights:

74
00:05:11.861 --> 00:05:20.479
Writes: scan events at 200M per day are ~2,300 per second average, with hub shift peaks ~10 times that.

75
00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:26.370
That is manageable, but every scan must be reliable even from sites with poor connectivity.

76
00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:32.687
Reads: tracking reads dominate, at ~1,200 per second average with spikes.

77
00:05:32.967 --> 00:05:37.447
They are served from read models and caches, not by recomputing timelines.

78
00:05:37.727 --> 00:05:43.231
Sorting throughput: a large automated hub sorts hundreds of thousands of parcels per shift.

79
00:05:43.511 --> 00:05:48.973
The sorter controller must decide each parcel's chute in milliseconds as it passes the scanner.

80
00:05:50.323 --> 00:05:51.197
Part 2.

81
00:05:51.477 --> 00:05:52.437
Requirements.

82
00:05:53.687 --> 00:05:54.541
Functional.

83
00:05:54.821 --> 00:06:06.021
Shippers create shipments via API (single or bulk) with consignee address, weight, dimensions, payment mode (prepaid or COD), declared value, and service type.

84
00:06:06.301 --> 00:06:11.165
They receive an AWB number (air waybill or tracking ID) and a label.

85
00:06:11.445 --> 00:06:15.775
Serviceability and TAT checks per origin, destination, service.

86
00:06:16.055 --> 00:06:20.023
Pickup scheduling and first-mile collection with scan-based handover.

87
00:06:20.303 --> 00:06:27.791
Hub processing: inbound, sort, bag, outbound, with exception handling (damaged, misrouted, unreadable labels).

88
00:06:29.141 --> 00:06:35.499
Line-haul trips with manifests, vehicle tracking and e-way bills where required (VERIFY thresholds).

89
00:06:35.779 --> 00:06:43.629
Last-mile delivery with OTP or signature proof, COD collection (cash or UPI), failed-attempt reasons.

90
00:06:43.909 --> 00:06:51.717
NDR workflow: notify the shipper and customer, take instructions (reattempt, change address, return), and re-attempt.

91
00:06:51.997 --> 00:06:55.923
RTO: return undelivered parcels to origin with tracking.

92
00:06:56.203 --> 00:06:59.659
Reverse pickups for customer returns, with doorstep checks.

93
00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:05.809
COD remittance to shippers on schedule; invoicing; weight dispute handling.

94
00:07:06.089 --> 00:07:11.998
Tracking pages, webhooks to shippers, and SMS or WhatsApp notifications to consignees.

95
00:07:13.348 --> 00:07:14.457
Non-functional.

96
00:07:14.737 --> 00:07:16.103
Here is a quick table:

97
00:07:16.383 --> 00:07:17.748
Attribute, and Target.

98
00:07:18.028 --> 00:07:19.415
Scan reliability:

99
00:07:19.695 --> 00:07:23.492
No scan lost; offline-first capture at hubs and in the field.

100
00:07:23.772 --> 00:07:24.860
Traceability:

101
00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.575
Every parcel's custody chain complete: who had it, when, where.

102
00:07:28.855 --> 00:07:29.772
Latency:

103
00:07:30.052 --> 00:07:35.748
Sorter decisions 50 ms per parcel; API shipment creation 300 ms.

104
00:07:36.028 --> 00:07:37.223
Tracking freshness:

105
00:07:37.503 --> 00:07:39.700
A scan visible in tracking within a minute.

106
00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:41.047
Availability:

107
00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:45.807
Hub operations continue during central outages (local edge systems).

108
00:07:46.087 --> 00:07:47.281
Money accuracy:

109
00:07:47.561 --> 00:07:52.703
COD collected = COD remitted (minus fees), reconciled daily.

110
00:07:54.053 --> 00:07:54.949
Part 3.

111
00:07:55.229 --> 00:07:56.765
High-Level Architecture.

112
00:07:58.015 --> 00:07:59.359
Here is the diagram.

113
00:08:03.209 --> 00:08:04.275
Key decisions:

114
00:08:05.625 --> 00:08:06.991
Here is a quick table:

115
00:08:07.271 --> 00:08:09.041
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

116
00:08:09.321 --> 00:08:10.388
Shipment state.

117
00:08:10.668 --> 00:08:11.500
Choice:

118
00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:17.625
Event-sourced: every scan is an immutable event; the current state and timeline are projections.

119
00:08:17.905 --> 00:08:18.588
Why:

120
00:08:18.868 --> 00:08:21.833
Custody chain, disputes, analytics (Ch 15).

121
00:08:22.113 --> 00:08:23.351
Facility systems.

122
00:08:23.631 --> 00:08:24.463
Choice:

123
00:08:24.743 --> 00:08:29.265
Edge nodes at hubs with local databases and sort plans; sync to central.

124
00:08:29.545 --> 00:08:30.228
Why:

125
00:08:30.508 --> 00:08:33.367
Hubs must keep sorting when the WAN is down.

126
00:08:33.647 --> 00:08:34.628
Mobile apps.

127
00:08:34.908 --> 00:08:35.740
Choice:

128
00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.732
Offline-first with local queues and idempotent event IDs.

129
00:08:40.012 --> 00:08:40.695
Why:

130
00:08:40.975 --> 00:08:42.660
Poor connectivity in the field.

131
00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:43.815
Tracking.

132
00:08:44.095 --> 00:08:44.927
Choice:

133
00:08:45.207 --> 00:08:49.985
Separate read models (per AWB timeline, per shipper views) with caches.

134
00:08:50.265 --> 00:08:50.948
Why:

135
00:08:51.228 --> 00:08:53.020
Read-heavy, public-facing.

136
00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:54.132
Planning.

137
00:08:54.412 --> 00:08:55.244
Choice:

138
00:08:55.524 --> 00:09:00.793
Batch optimisation (nightly or weekly) producing plans consumed by operations.

139
00:09:01.073 --> 00:09:01.756
Why:

140
00:09:02.036 --> 00:09:04.511
Complex solvers off the real-time path.

141
00:09:05.861 --> 00:09:06.778
Part 4.

142
00:09:07.058 --> 00:09:08.935
How It Works, A Parcel's Journey.

143
00:09:10.185 --> 00:09:27.060
Follow AWB 123456789: a 1,299 rupeesCOD order from a D2C brand's warehouse in Bhiwandi (Maharashtra) to a customer in Jorhat (Assam).

144
00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:29.178
Booking.

145
00:09:29.458 --> 00:09:45.501
API call: the brand's order system (or its shipping aggregator) calls Create Shipment: consignee name, phone, address, pincode, weight 0.8 kg, dimensions, COD 1,299, rupeesservice surface express.

146
00:09:45.781 --> 00:09:52.117
Serviceability: is pincode 785001 served for COD on this service?

147
00:09:52.397 --> 00:09:54.871
What is the TAT from the origin cluster?

148
00:09:55.151 --> 00:09:59.695
The answer comes from a precomputed TAT matrix (§5.2).

149
00:09:59.975 --> 00:10:06.247
Address intelligence (§6.1): parse the free-text address;. geocode it to coordinates;.

150
00:10:07.597 --> 00:10:13.848
validate that the pincode matches the location;. assign the delivery centre and a delivery zone or beat.

151
00:10:14.128 --> 00:10:23.003
If the address is poor quality (no landmark, pincode mismatch), flag it for early correction via the consignee (a WhatsApp or SMS link).

152
00:10:23.283 --> 00:10:39.411
Routing: the booking service assigns the AWB, computes the planned route (origin hub, then Mumbai sort hub, then Kolkata gateway, then Guwahati hub, then Jorhat delivery centre), and generates the label with barcode, AWB and sort codes.

153
00:10:40.761 --> 00:10:45.390
Response: the shipment is created with a promised date and returned to the brand.

154
00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.910
The event SHIPMENTCREATED is recorded.

155
00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:50.668
Pickup and first mile.

156
00:10:50.948 --> 00:11:02.169
Pickup planning: pickup requests are grouped by client location and time window, and assigned to pickup vehicles by area (a VRP with pickup windows; Ch 47).

157
00:11:02.449 --> 00:11:07.569
At the warehouse: the pickup executive scans each parcel's AWB in the app.

158
00:11:07.849 --> 00:11:13.545
The handover manifest is signed digitally by both parties, and the scans become PICKEDUP events.

159
00:11:13.825 --> 00:11:16.812
Parcels expected but not handed over are flagged.

160
00:11:17.092 --> 00:11:20.868
Parcels scanned but not booked are rejected, or booked on the spot.

161
00:11:22.218 --> 00:11:28.085
To the first hub: the pickup vehicle returns to the origin hub, and the parcels are inbound-scanned.

162
00:11:29.435 --> 00:11:30.544
Hub sortation.

163
00:11:30.824 --> 00:11:37.928
Induction: parcels are inducted onto an automated sorter (cross-belt or shoe sorter; VERIFY equipment types).

164
00:11:38.208 --> 00:11:46.251
Scan tunnel: it reads barcodes, captures dimensions and weight (DWS: dimensioning, weighing, scanning) and takes images.

165
00:11:46.531 --> 00:12:00.867
Chute decision: the sorter controller looks up the AWB's next destination in the sort plan (§6.3), for example, bag for Kolkata gateway, and sends the parcel to the corresponding chute within milliseconds.

166
00:12:01.147 --> 00:12:03.429
Bagging: at the chute, parcels are bagged.

167
00:12:03.709 --> 00:12:08.189
A bag gets its own barcode, and the bag, parcel mapping is recorded (BAGGED).

168
00:12:09.539 --> 00:12:14.574
Manifest: bags are loaded onto an outbound trip's vehicle and scanned (BAGLOADED).

169
00:12:14.854 --> 00:12:17.307
The trip manifest lists bags and parcels.

170
00:12:17.587 --> 00:12:38.003
Exceptions: unreadable labels, then manual scan station;. damaged parcels, then photo and exception flow;. weight discrepancy (declared 0.8 kg, measured 1.6 kg), then the billing weight is updated with evidence images (§6.7);. misrouted parcels, then re-sort.

171
00:12:39.353 --> 00:12:40.249
Line-haul.

172
00:12:40.529 --> 00:12:49.276
Trip plan: a trip Mumbai hub, then Kolkata gateway, departing 22:00, is planned from the trip plan (§6.4).

173
00:12:49.556 --> 00:12:54.975
The vehicle and driver are assigned, with GPS tracking via a device or driver app.

174
00:12:55.255 --> 00:13:07.159
Compliance: the e-way bill is generated for the consignment where required (GST rules on goods value and movement; VERIFY thresholds and consolidated e-way bill processes).

175
00:13:07.439 --> 00:13:13.583
Toll crossings via FASTag can serve as tracking signals (VERIFY data availability).

176
00:13:13.863 --> 00:13:18.748
In transit: trip events are recorded: departed, checkpoints, delays, arrival.

177
00:13:19.028 --> 00:13:23.444
The ETA to the next hub is predicted continuously (§6.5).

178
00:13:24.794 --> 00:13:27.183
Arrival: bags are unloaded and scanned.

179
00:13:27.463 --> 00:13:30.301
Missing bags against the manifest trigger an exception.

180
00:13:30.581 --> 00:13:31.626
Onward legs:

181
00:13:31.906 --> 00:13:36.770
Kolkata gateway, then Guwahati hub (by road), then Guwahati, then Jorhat.

182
00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:41.935
Each hub repeats inbound, sort (to the next hub or delivery centre) and outbound.

183
00:13:43.285 --> 00:13:44.181
Last mile.

184
00:13:44.461 --> 00:14:02.744
At the delivery centre: the bag is opened and parcels inbound-scanned (ATDELIVERYCENTRE);. the beat planning assigns parcels to riders by zone, with sequencing (route optimisation);. riders scan parcels into their bag (OUTFORDELIVERY);. the consignee gets an SMS or WhatsApp:

185
00:14:03.024 --> 00:14:07.739
Out for delivery today, 1,299 rupeesto be paid.

186
00:14:09.089 --> 00:14:25.281
Delivery: the rider navigates and calls the customer (masked number) if needed;. collects COD in cash or via UPI QR (dynamic QR for the exact amount, confirming payment in the app);. captures OTP or signature (DELIVERED).

187
00:14:25.561 --> 00:14:26.649
Failed attempt:

188
00:14:27.999 --> 00:14:42.975
the rider records a reason (customer unavailable, refused, address not found, asked to reschedule);. an NDR event goes to the NDR workflow (§4.6);. the parcel returns to the delivery centre.

189
00:14:43.255 --> 00:14:55.244
End of day: the rider reconciles cash at the delivery centre: cash handed over plus UPI collected = COD due for delivered parcels;. discrepancies are recorded against the rider;.

190
00:14:56.594 --> 00:14:59.218
the delivery centre deposits cash to the bank.

191
00:14:59.498 --> 00:15:02.655
Deposits are reconciled (§5.6).

192
00:15:04.005 --> 00:15:05.520
NDR and RTO.

193
00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:10.728
Notify: the shipper is notified of the NDR (API webhook and panel).

194
00:15:11.008 --> 00:15:18.965
The consignee is contacted by automated IVR or WhatsApp to confirm intent and preferred time, or to correct the address.

195
00:15:19.245 --> 00:15:27.181
Instructions: from the shipper or consignee, for example, reattempt tomorrow, reattempt with an updated address, or return.

196
00:15:27.461 --> 00:15:31.984
Retry: re-attempts up to a limit (for example, 3; per shipper contract).

197
00:15:32.264 --> 00:15:41.843
RTO: after the limit, or if the customer refuses, the parcel is marked RTO and travels back through the network to the origin (RTOINTRANSIT...

198
00:15:42.123 --> 00:15:43.147
RTODELIVERED).

199
00:15:43.427 --> 00:15:46.733
The shipper receives it back and is charged RTO fees.

200
00:15:48.083 --> 00:15:56.510
Measurement: the RTO rate by pincode, shipper, payment type and reason feeds shippers' COD risk models (I02 L2).

201
00:15:57.860 --> 00:15:59.652
COD remittance and billing.

202
00:15:59.932 --> 00:16:00.828
Remittance:

203
00:16:01.108 --> 00:16:11.647
COD collected for delivered parcels is remitted to the shipper on an agreed schedule (for example, T+2 after delivery), net of shipping charges if agreed.

204
00:16:11.927 --> 00:16:15.297
The remittance report lists every AWB with amounts.

205
00:16:15.577 --> 00:16:32.281
Billing: shipping charges are based on chargeable weight, the greater of actual and volumetric weight (L times B times H or divisor; VERIFY divisors per service), zone (origin, destination distance class), COD fee, and RTO charges.

206
00:16:32.561 --> 00:16:35.527
Weight disputes: the shipper may dispute a weight change.

207
00:16:35.807 --> 00:16:39.988
Evidence (DWS images, measurements) supports resolution.

208
00:16:41.338 --> 00:16:42.298
Part 5.

209
00:16:42.578 --> 00:16:44.114
Core Subsystems in Depth.

210
00:16:45.364 --> 00:16:46.644
Shipment event store.

211
00:16:46.924 --> 00:17:02.007
Every action is an event: created, pickedup, hubin(hub), sorted(chute), bagged(bag), loaded(trip), tripdeparted, triparrived, hubout, outfordelivery(rider), deliveryattempted(reason), delivered(pod), rtoinitiated, and so on.

212
00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:13.828
Each event carries: the AWB (or bag or trip ID, with bag events expanding to their parcels);. location (facility or GPS);. the actor (user or device);.

213
00:17:15.178 --> 00:17:23.669
device timestamp and server receipt time;. a unique event ID generated on the device, which makes offline retries idempotent.

214
00:17:23.949 --> 00:17:33.911
Storage: an append-only event log, partitioned by AWB (Kafka + a store like Cassandra, or partitioned PostgreSQL for recent data).

215
00:17:34.191 --> 00:17:40.762
Projections: current status per AWB;. timeline for tracking;. location of every bag and trip;.

216
00:17:42.112 --> 00:17:46.229
facility inventory (what's physically in the hub now);. exception queues.

217
00:17:46.509 --> 00:17:50.797
Out-of-order events: device clocks and offline sync deliver events late.

218
00:17:51.077 --> 00:17:59.973
Projections order events by device timestamp, with sanity rules (can't be delivered before being out for delivery), and flag inconsistencies.

219
00:18:01.323 --> 00:18:03.606
Serviceability and TAT matrix.

220
00:18:03.886 --> 00:18:16.579
Serviceability: per pincode, which services (surface, express, air), COD allowed and the COD amount limit, reverse pickup allowed, heavy parcels, and ODA (out-of-delivery-area) surcharges.

221
00:18:16.859 --> 00:18:21.851
TAT: for each (origin cluster, destination pincode, service), the promised days.

222
00:18:22.131 --> 00:18:31.283
Computed from: the network route;. trip schedules and cut-off times;. historical performance: the P90 transit of the lane, not the average.

223
00:18:32.633 --> 00:18:35.577
It is updated weekly or when the network changes.

224
00:18:35.857 --> 00:18:43.708
Serving: a precomputed table in a low-latency key-value store, answering millions of API checks per day in milliseconds.

225
00:18:45.058 --> 00:18:46.274
Hub edge systems.

226
00:18:46.554 --> 00:18:52.250
Why: a large hub runs 24 times 7 with sorters, scanners and hundreds of staff.

227
00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:55.175
A WAN outage must not stop sorting.

228
00:18:55.455 --> 00:19:09.706
Edge node: a local database with the sort plan, expected inbound (manifests), and parcels currently in the hub;. local services for scanning apps and sorter controllers;. an event outbox that syncs to central when connected.

229
00:19:11.056 --> 00:19:18.480
Sort plan distribution: a new sort plan version is pushed from central before shift start and activated at a set time.

230
00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.875
The sorter controller keeps the active version in memory.

231
00:19:23.225 --> 00:19:24.505
Line-haul TMS.

232
00:19:24.785 --> 00:19:28.731
Trips: planned (from the trip plan) and ad hoc (extra capacity).

233
00:19:29.011 --> 00:19:36.926
Each trip has a vehicle (own, leased or market vehicle), driver, route, schedule, capacity, and seal numbers for security.

234
00:19:37.206 --> 00:19:38.358
Vehicle tracking:

235
00:19:38.638 --> 00:19:46.510
GPS from devices or driver apps, geofences for hubs, and alerts for delays, route deviations and long stops.

236
00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:51.355
Loading: bags and pallets are assigned to trips by destination and cut-off.

237
00:19:51.635 --> 00:19:57.310
Load planning (§6.6) maximises fill while respecting unloading order.

238
00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:03.435
Cost: per trip, fuel, tolls and driver payments; cost per kg per lane is a key metric.

239
00:20:04.785 --> 00:20:05.681
Last mile.

240
00:20:05.961 --> 00:20:11.103
Covered in Ch 47: rider assignment, route sequencing and live tracking.

241
00:20:11.383 --> 00:20:12.940
Parcel-specific additions:

242
00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:16.847
Beat planning: delivery centres divide their area into beats.

243
00:20:17.127 --> 00:20:22.631
Parcels are assigned by geocoded location, and each rider's sequence is optimised.

244
00:20:22.911 --> 00:20:26.559
Delivery windows: customer-selected slots for some shippers.

245
00:20:26.839 --> 00:20:28.012
POD capture:

246
00:20:28.292 --> 00:20:38.489
OTP, signature or photo, with geotag validation (the delivery location is near the address geocode; mismatches are flagged for fake-delivery checks).

247
00:20:39.839 --> 00:20:41.503
COD reconciliation.

248
00:20:41.783 --> 00:20:48.247
The chain: rider, then delivery centre, then bank deposit, then central account, then remittance to shipper.

249
00:20:48.527 --> 00:21:01.391
Ledgers: per rider (cash due, submitted), per delivery centre (cash on hand, deposited), per shipper (COD collected, remitted), and UPI collections reconciled with payment gateway settlements.

250
00:21:01.671 --> 00:21:07.602
Controls: daily reconciliation;. cash limits per rider and per delivery centre (security);.

251
00:21:08.952 --> 00:21:13.816
aging of unremitted amounts;. dispute handling (Ch 45 patterns).

252
00:21:15.166 --> 00:21:16.745
Tracking and notifications.

253
00:21:17.025 --> 00:21:29.590
Read models: per AWB, the latest status, the timeline of customer-friendly events, and the expected delivery date;. updated from events within seconds, and cached at the edge for public tracking pages.

254
00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:36.569
Webhooks to shippers on status changes, with retries and signatures, so shippers stop polling (Ch 40).

255
00:21:36.849 --> 00:21:42.971
Consignee notifications: shipped, out for delivery, delivered, attempted (with NDR options).

256
00:21:44.321 --> 00:21:45.260
Part 6.

257
00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:46.500
Algorithms.

258
00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:49.542
Address parsing and geocoding.

259
00:21:49.822 --> 00:22:00.318
Indian addresses: free text, mixed languages and scripts, landmarks (near Hanuman temple), missing or wrong pincodes, local area names that don't appear on maps.

260
00:22:00.598 --> 00:22:01.494
Pipeline:

261
00:22:01.774 --> 00:22:05.187
Normalisation: case, abbreviations, transliteration.

262
00:22:05.467 --> 00:22:14.875
Parsing into components (house, building, street, locality, landmark, city, pincode) with a sequence labelling model trained on labelled addresses.

263
00:22:16.225 --> 00:22:22.817
Geocoding: match locality and landmark names against a gazetteer, and historical delivery coordinates.

264
00:22:23.097 --> 00:22:27.513
The best source is where riders actually delivered to similar addresses before.

265
00:22:27.793 --> 00:22:29.009
Confidence scoring.

266
00:22:29.289 --> 00:22:33.577
Pincode validation: does the geocode fall in the pincode polygon?

267
00:22:33.857 --> 00:22:41.623
Clustering: delivered GPS points for the same normalised address are clustered to learn exact locations over time.

268
00:22:42.973 --> 00:22:53.703
Uses: delivery centre assignment, beat planning, fake-delivery checks, address-quality nudges to consignees, and reseller detection for shippers (I02 L4).

269
00:22:55.053 --> 00:22:57.400
Network design (facility location).

270
00:22:57.680 --> 00:23:06.875
The question: where should hubs and delivery centres be, and which pincodes should each serve, to minimise transport and facility costs while meeting TATs?

271
00:23:07.155 --> 00:23:20.019
Model: a mixed-integer linear program: binary variables for opening facilities;. assignment variables for pincode, then delivery centre, then hub;. flow variables on lanes;. capacity constraints;.

272
00:23:21.369 --> 00:23:27.662
TAT constraints;. objective: fixed costs + variable handling costs + transport costs.

273
00:23:27.942 --> 00:23:34.449
Scale: solved with commercial or open solvers, with decomposition and heuristics for large instances.

274
00:23:34.729 --> 00:23:39.102
Run for strategic planning (quarterly), with scenarios for demand growth.

275
00:23:40.452 --> 00:23:41.433
Sort plans.

276
00:23:41.713 --> 00:23:47.943
The input: for each hub, destinations (next hubs or delivery centres) mapped to chutes on the sorter.

277
00:23:48.223 --> 00:23:55.092
There are more destinations than chutes, so some chutes combine destinations (mixed bags sorted again downstream).

278
00:23:55.372 --> 00:24:06.977
The optimisation: assign high-volume destinations to dedicated chutes;. group low-volume destinations whose next hop is the same;. balance chute load so no chute overflows;.

279
00:24:08.327 --> 00:24:12.445
respect physical constraints (chute positions near their outbound docks).

280
00:24:12.725 --> 00:24:19.658
Solved by a greedy heuristic plus local search, or by integer programming, per shift pattern and volume forecast.

281
00:24:19.938 --> 00:24:24.802
Versioning: plans are versioned, pushed to the edge, and activated at shift boundaries.

282
00:24:26.152 --> 00:24:27.709
Trip planning (middle mile).

283
00:24:27.989 --> 00:24:43.413
The problem: given forecast volume between hub pairs and time windows (hub cut-offs, arrival needed for downstream connections), choose the vehicles and routes (direct, or multi-stop milk runs) to minimise cost while meeting TATs.

284
00:24:43.693 --> 00:24:47.320
Model: a vehicle routing or service network design problem.

285
00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:51.099
Heuristics construct routes, and local search improves them.

286
00:24:51.379 --> 00:24:53.213
Capacity by weight and volume.

287
00:24:53.493 --> 00:25:01.280
Daily adjustments: dynamic dispatch: if actual volume exceeds plan, add vehicles; if lower, consolidate trips.

288
00:25:02.630 --> 00:25:04.273
ETA and delay prediction.

289
00:25:04.553 --> 00:25:15.091
Shipment ETA: predict delivery date and time from the current location, the remaining route, trip schedules, historical lane performance, hub backlogs, weather and festivals.

290
00:25:15.371 --> 00:25:19.318
A gradient-boosted model or a survival model per remaining leg.

291
00:25:19.598 --> 00:25:20.473
Trip ETA:

292
00:25:20.753 --> 00:25:27.579
GPS progress, road network travel times (I05 §6.3), driver stops.

293
00:25:27.859 --> 00:25:37.673
At-risk shipments (likely to miss the promise) are flagged in the control tower for intervention: prioritise them in sorting, or move them to a faster service.

294
00:25:39.023 --> 00:25:41.199
Load planning (3D bin packing).

295
00:25:41.479 --> 00:25:48.071
The problem: pack bags, cartons or pallets into a truck, maximising volume and weight utilisation.

296
00:25:48.351 --> 00:25:58.121
Constraints: weight distribution;. stackability and fragility;. unloading order (last stop's cargo loaded first, that is, last in, first out by stop).

297
00:25:59.471 --> 00:26:07.898
Methods: heuristics (layer-building, extreme-point placement, first-fit decreasing by volume), with visual loading plans for staff.

298
00:26:08.178 --> 00:26:10.973
Measure: fill rate per trip, a key cost lever.

299
00:26:12.323 --> 00:26:13.987
Weight discrepancy detection.

300
00:26:14.267 --> 00:26:19.472
Compare declared weight and dimensions with DWS measurements at first hub scan.

301
00:26:19.752 --> 00:26:23.037
Chargeable weight is max(actual, volumetric).

302
00:26:23.317 --> 00:26:25.387
Flag discrepancies above tolerance.

303
00:26:25.667 --> 00:26:28.525
Use evidence images and measurement confidence.

304
00:26:28.805 --> 00:26:32.176
Pattern detection: shippers who consistently under-declare.

305
00:26:32.456 --> 00:26:40.371
SKU-level learned weights (this product ID usually weighs 1.4 kg) help predict and pre-empt disputes.

306
00:26:41.721 --> 00:26:44.494
Carrier allocation (for shipping aggregators).

307
00:26:44.774 --> 00:27:07.302
Aggregators (platforms like Shiprocket that route shipments across many carriers) choose a carrier per shipment: score each carrier by price, predicted TAT, delivery success rate and RTO rate for the destination pincode, COD remittance speed, and pickup reliability at the origin;. optimise the shipper's objective (cost versus speed versus success).

308
00:27:07.582 --> 00:27:12.105
Learning: multi-armed bandit exploration keeps estimates fresh per lane.

309
00:27:13.455 --> 00:27:15.396
Lost and stuck shipment detection.

310
00:27:15.676 --> 00:27:23.441
Expected next event per shipment state (for example, after hubout for trip X, expect triparrived at Y by time T).

311
00:27:23.721 --> 00:27:26.559
Missing events beyond a threshold, then an exception.

312
00:27:26.839 --> 00:27:36.375
Facility reconciliation: expected inbound (from manifests) versus actual scans; parcels in the hub inventory not scanned for N hours.

313
00:27:36.655 --> 00:27:40.239
Searches: trigger hub searches (with last-known locations).

314
00:27:40.519 --> 00:27:42.887
Unresolved cases become lost claims.

315
00:27:44.237 --> 00:27:46.178
Supply chain planning for shippers.

316
00:27:46.458 --> 00:27:53.754
Demand forecasting: hierarchical forecasts by SKU, location and week, with promotions and seasonality.

317
00:27:54.034 --> 00:27:56.359
Probabilistic outputs (quantiles).

318
00:27:56.639 --> 00:28:16.181
Inventory optimisation: safety stock per SKU per location = z times σ(demand during lead time), where z comes from the target service level;. multi-echelon optimisation (central warehouse + regional warehouses) places stock where it minimises total cost for a service level;.

319
00:28:17.531 --> 00:28:26.661
reorder points and quantities (EOQ with constraints) (I04 §6.8 uses the same logic for hospital supplies).

320
00:28:26.941 --> 00:28:31.720
Allocation of stock across marketplaces and warehouses by forecast demand.

321
00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:35.904
Replenishment plans feed purchase orders and inbound logistics.

322
00:28:37.254 --> 00:28:38.193
