They are masked on labels where possible (phone masked, calls via proxy), restricted in apps, retained per policy, and subject to the DPDP Act (VERIFY).

455
00:33:31.792 --> 00:33:34.949
Cash handling: limits, secure deposits, audits.

456
00:33:35.229 --> 00:33:44.893
Custody and theft: seal numbers on trips, CCTV at hubs, scan-based custody, and investigation workflows for lost high-value parcels.

457
00:33:45.173 --> 00:33:46.197
Regulatory:

458
00:33:46.477 --> 00:33:50.595
GST e-way bills for goods movement above thresholds (VERIFY);.

459
00:33:51.945 --> 00:34:00.393
dangerous goods restrictions for air (lithium batteries; VERIFY IATA or DGCA rules);. labour and vehicle regulations.

460
00:34:00.673 --> 00:34:01.995
Client integrations:

461
00:34:02.275 --> 00:34:05.817
API keys and OAuth, webhook signatures, rate limits.

462
00:34:07.167 --> 00:34:08.020
Part 10.

463
00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:10.220
Reliability and Operations.

464
00:34:11.470 --> 00:34:15.779
Offline-first everywhere: scanners and apps store events locally and sync.

465
00:34:16.059 --> 00:34:17.574
Hubs run on edge nodes.

466
00:34:17.854 --> 00:34:20.905
Central outages delay tracking, not operations.

467
00:34:21.185 --> 00:34:33.963
Peak readiness (festive sales, I02): volume forecasts per lane;. temporary hubs and extra trips;. extra riders;. sort plans for peak;. pre-alerts from shippers (expected volumes);.

468
00:34:35.313 --> 00:34:37.831
capacity caps per shipper to protect the network.

469
00:34:38.111 --> 00:34:48.201
Control tower watches backlogs per hub and shift, delayed trips, at-risk shipments and NDR rates, with playbooks (divert trips, add shifts, move volume to air).

470
00:34:48.481 --> 00:34:53.729
Data quality: scans with wrong facility codes or clock errors corrupt projections.

471
00:34:54.009 --> 00:34:57.295
Validation rules at capture, plus reconciliation.

472
00:34:57.575 --> 00:35:04.359
Weather and disruption: floods and strikes reroute lanes; update the TAT matrix and shipper communication.

473
00:35:05.709 --> 00:35:06.711
Part 11.

474
00:35:06.991 --> 00:35:08.015
Key Trade-offs.

475
00:35:09.265 --> 00:35:10.631
Here is a quick table:

476
00:35:10.911 --> 00:35:13.428
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

477
00:35:13.708 --> 00:35:14.775
Shipment state.

478
00:35:15.055 --> 00:35:15.887
Chosen:

479
00:35:16.167 --> 00:35:17.233
Event-sourced.

480
00:35:17.513 --> 00:35:18.687
Accepted cost:

481
00:35:18.967 --> 00:35:21.527
Projection complexity; out-of-order handling.

482
00:35:21.807 --> 00:35:22.831
Facilities.

483
00:35:23.111 --> 00:35:23.943
Chosen:

484
00:35:24.223 --> 00:35:25.289
Edge autonomy.

485
00:35:25.569 --> 00:35:26.743
Accepted cost:

486
00:35:27.023 --> 00:35:29.135
Sync conflicts and version management.

487
00:35:29.415 --> 00:35:30.759
TAT promises.

488
00:35:31.039 --> 00:35:31.871
Chosen:

489
00:35:32.151 --> 00:35:33.751
P90-based by lane.

490
00:35:34.031 --> 00:35:35.204
Accepted cost:

491
00:35:35.484 --> 00:35:38.193
Conservative promises versus competitors.

492
00:35:38.473 --> 00:35:39.455
Sort plans.

493
00:35:39.735 --> 00:35:40.567
Chosen:

494
00:35:40.847 --> 00:35:42.468
Batch-optimised per shift.

495
00:35:42.748 --> 00:35:43.921
Accepted cost:

496
00:35:44.201 --> 00:35:46.420
Less adaptive to sudden volume shifts.

497
00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:47.575
Tracking.

498
00:35:47.855 --> 00:35:48.687
Chosen:

499
00:35:48.967 --> 00:35:50.545
Read models + webhooks.

500
00:35:50.825 --> 00:35:51.999
Accepted cost:

501
00:35:52.279 --> 00:35:54.284
Eventual consistency (seconds).

502
00:35:54.564 --> 00:35:55.396
Planning.

503
00:35:55.676 --> 00:35:56.508
Chosen:

504
00:35:56.788 --> 00:35:58.601
Heuristics + MILP offline.

505
00:35:58.881 --> 00:36:00.055
Accepted cost:

506
00:36:00.335 --> 00:36:02.511
Not globally optimal in real time.

507
00:36:03.861 --> 00:36:04.799
Part 12.

508
00:36:05.079 --> 00:36:06.423
Interview Q and A.

509
00:36:07.673 --> 00:36:08.505
Beginner.

510
00:36:08.785 --> 00:36:09.809
Question 1.

511
00:36:10.089 --> 00:36:12.991
Walk through a parcel's journey in an express network.

512
00:36:13.271 --> 00:36:14.636
Thirty-second answer:

513
00:36:14.916 --> 00:36:39.087
Booking via API (AWB, label, promised date), then pickup scan, then first hub inbound, then sortation into bags by next destination, then line-haul trips between hubs, then destination delivery centre, then rider out for delivery, then delivered with OTP and COD, or failed attempt, then NDR, then reattempt or RTO; every step is a scan event.

514
00:36:40.437 --> 00:36:41.439
Question 2.

515
00:36:41.719 --> 00:36:44.237
What is volumetric weight and why does it matter?

516
00:36:44.517 --> 00:36:45.882
Thirty-second answer:

517
00:36:46.162 --> 00:37:01.010
Light but bulky parcels take space, so carriers charge the greater of actual weight and volumetric weight (length times breadth times height ÷ a divisor), which affects shipper costs and requires measurement to resolve disputes.

518
00:37:02.360 --> 00:37:03.405
Question 3.

519
00:37:03.685 --> 00:37:05.435
What happens when delivery fails?

520
00:37:05.715 --> 00:37:07.080
Thirty-second answer:

521
00:37:07.360 --> 00:37:21.632
The rider records a reason; an NDR is raised to the shipper and customer; instructions (reattempt, new address, return) are collected; the parcel is re-attempted up to a limit or returned to origin (RTO).

522
00:37:22.982 --> 00:37:24.049
Question 4.

523
00:37:24.329 --> 00:37:26.398
How does parcel tracking work at scale?

524
00:37:26.678 --> 00:37:28.043
Thirty-second answer:

525
00:37:28.323 --> 00:37:40.995
Every scan is an event; projections maintain the latest status and a customer-friendly timeline per AWB in a cache; tracking pages read those; shippers receive webhooks instead of polling.

526
00:37:42.345 --> 00:37:43.348
Intermediate.

527
00:37:43.628 --> 00:37:44.737
Question 5.

528
00:37:45.017 --> 00:37:47.023
Why model shipments as event streams?

529
00:37:47.303 --> 00:37:48.668
Thirty-second answer:

530
00:37:48.948 --> 00:38:06.783
Every physical action is a fact with a time, place and actor; an append-only log gives a complete custody chain for disputes and analytics, supports offline devices with idempotent events, and lets different projections (status, timeline, facility inventory) serve different needs.

531
00:38:08.133 --> 00:38:09.242
Question 6.

532
00:38:09.522 --> 00:38:12.999
How do you compute and serve delivery promises (TAT)?

533
00:38:13.279 --> 00:38:14.645
Thirty-second answer:

534
00:38:14.925 --> 00:38:32.226
Per origin cluster, destination pincode and service, compute days from the route, trip schedules and cut-offs, calibrated to the lane's historical P90 transit; precompute into a key-value table for millisecond lookups; update with network changes and disruptions.

535
00:38:33.576 --> 00:38:34.664
Question 7.

536
00:38:34.944 --> 00:38:37.504
How do hubs keep working when the network is down?

537
00:38:37.784 --> 00:38:39.149
Thirty-second answer:

538
00:38:39.429 --> 00:38:51.248
Edge nodes at each hub hold sort plans, manifests and hub inventory locally, serve scanners and sorter controllers, and queue events in an outbox that syncs to central when connectivity returns.

539
00:38:52.598 --> 00:38:53.622
Question 8.

540
00:38:53.902 --> 00:38:56.590
How does COD money flow and get reconciled?

541
00:38:56.870 --> 00:38:58.235
Thirty-second answer:

542
00:38:58.515 --> 00:39:16.265
Riders collect cash or UPI, reconcile with the delivery centre daily, the delivery centre deposits to the bank, collections are reconciled with bank and gateway data, and remittances go to shippers per AWB on schedule, each step tracked in ledgers with limits and aging.

543
00:39:17.615 --> 00:39:18.681
Question 9.

544
00:39:18.961 --> 00:39:21.265
How would you handle messy Indian addresses?

545
00:39:21.545 --> 00:39:22.911
Thirty-second answer:

546
00:39:23.191 --> 00:39:38.913
Normalise and parse with a trained sequence model, geocode using a gazetteer and historical delivery coordinates, validate pincode against location, score confidence, and nudge consignees to correct low-confidence addresses before dispatch.

547
00:39:40.263 --> 00:39:41.031
Senior.

548
00:39:41.311 --> 00:39:42.357
Question 10.

549
00:39:42.637 --> 00:39:44.493
How do you design a hub's sort plan?

550
00:39:44.773 --> 00:39:46.138
Thirty-second answer:

551
00:39:46.418 --> 00:40:04.487
Map destinations to chutes: dedicated chutes for high-volume destinations, grouped chutes for low-volume ones sharing a next hop, balanced chute loads, physical constraints near outbound docks, optimised per shift from forecasts, versioned and pushed to the edge for activation at shift change.

552
00:40:05.837 --> 00:40:06.968
Question 11.

553
00:40:07.248 --> 00:40:09.509
How would you detect lost or stuck shipments?

554
00:40:09.789 --> 00:40:11.155
Thirty-second answer:

555
00:40:11.435 --> 00:40:28.203
Define the expected next event per state with time windows, flag shipments missing them, reconcile manifests versus inbound scans per facility and trip, age hub inventory without scans, trigger searches with last-known locations, and escalate to lost claims.

556
00:40:29.553 --> 00:40:30.641
Question 12.

557
00:40:30.921 --> 00:40:32.798
How do you plan middle-mile trips?

558
00:40:33.078 --> 00:40:34.443
Thirty-second answer:

559
00:40:34.723 --> 00:40:53.305
From lane volume forecasts and hub time windows, solve a vehicle routing or service network design problem with capacity by weight and volume, using heuristics and local search; adjust daily by adding or consolidating trips based on actual volumes; measure fill rate and on-time performance.

560
00:40:54.655 --> 00:40:55.935
Question 13.

561
00:40:56.215 --> 00:40:59.031
How would a shipping aggregator choose carriers per shipment?

562
00:40:59.311 --> 00:41:00.676
Thirty-second answer:

563
00:41:00.956 --> 00:41:17.297
Score each carrier on price, predicted TAT, delivery success and RTO rates for the destination pincode, remittance speed and pickup reliability at origin; optimise the shipper's objective, with bandit exploration to keep lane estimates fresh.

564
00:41:18.647 --> 00:41:19.842
Question 14.

565
00:41:20.122 --> 00:41:23.215
How do you predict and prevent missed delivery promises?

566
00:41:23.495 --> 00:41:24.861
Thirty-second answer:

567
00:41:25.141 --> 00:41:40.863
Predict ETA per remaining leg from lane history, trip progress, hub backlogs, weather and festivals; flag at-risk shipments in the control tower; intervene by prioritising sorting, switching to faster lanes or proactive customer communication.

568
00:41:42.213 --> 00:41:43.109
Architect.

569
00:41:43.389 --> 00:41:44.584
Question 15.

570
00:41:44.864 --> 00:41:47.872
Design an express logistics platform end to end.

571
00:41:48.152 --> 00:41:49.517
Thirty-second answer:

572
00:41:49.797 --> 00:42:02.811
Client APIs for booking with serviceability, TAT and address intelligence; offline-first field apps and hub edge nodes producing idempotent scan events; an event store with projections for tracking,

573
00:42:03.091 --> 00:42:20.947
facility inventory and exceptions; TMS for trips and e-way bills; last-mile dispatch; NDR or RTO and COD workflows; planning (network, sort, trips, forecasts) offline; a control tower from streaming aggregates; a lakehouse for analytics and ML.

574
00:42:22.297 --> 00:42:23.491
Question 16.

575
00:42:23.771 --> 00:42:25.883
How would you decide where to build new hubs?

576
00:42:26.163 --> 00:42:27.529
Thirty-second answer:

577
00:42:27.809 --> 00:42:43.830
A facility location MILP with open or assign or flow variables, capacities and TAT constraints, minimising fixed plus variable costs across demand scenarios; validate with simulation, real estate constraints and phased rollout.

578
00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:46.524
Question 17.

579
00:42:46.804 --> 00:42:49.215
How do you prepare the network for a festive sale?

580
00:42:49.495 --> 00:42:50.860
Thirty-second answer:

581
00:42:51.140 --> 00:43:05.369
Volume forecasts per lane from shippers' pre-alerts and history, temporary hubs, peak sort plans, extra trips and riders, shipper capacity caps, TAT adjustments, control-tower playbooks and daily reviews during the peak.

582
00:43:06.719 --> 00:43:07.893
Question 18.

583
00:43:08.173 --> 00:43:11.693
How would you build supply chain planning for a D2C brand?

584
00:43:11.973 --> 00:43:13.338
Thirty-second answer:

585
00:43:13.618 --> 00:43:34.973
Hierarchical probabilistic demand forecasts by SKU and location, safety stock from service levels and lead-time variability, multi-echelon placement across warehouses, replenishment plans into purchase orders and inbound logistics, and allocation across marketplaces, measured by fill rate, inventory turns and stock-outs.

586
00:43:36.323 --> 00:43:37.560
Question 19.

587
00:43:37.840 --> 00:43:40.229
What makes logistics data platforms tricky?

588
00:43:40.509 --> 00:43:41.875
Thirty-second answer:

589
00:43:42.155 --> 00:43:58.965
Events come from offline devices with unreliable clocks, physical-world errors (wrong scans, missing scans), bag and trip hierarchies, and long-running shipments, so validation, reconciliation, idempotency and careful projections matter as much as scale.

590
00:44:00.315 --> 00:44:01.425
Question 20.

591
00:44:01.705 --> 00:44:05.929
Compare a 3PL network with a marketplace's captive logistics.

592
00:44:06.209 --> 00:44:07.574
Thirty-second answer:

593
00:44:07.854 --> 00:44:29.401
A 3PL serves many shippers with varied data quality, contracts and SLAs, so APIs, serviceability, billing and disputes are central; a captive network (like a marketplace's own) integrates tightly with order and inventory systems and can optimise end to end, both share hubs, sort plans, trips, last mile and event tracking.

594
00:44:30.751 --> 00:44:31.796
Part 13.

595
00:44:32.076 --> 00:44:32.844
Summary.

596
00:44:34.094 --> 00:44:36.739
A parcel's life is a chain of custody events.

597
00:44:37.019 --> 00:44:41.883
An event store with projections gives tracking, facility inventory and exceptions.

598
00:44:42.163 --> 00:44:48.051
Hubs are autonomous at the edge, running sort plans that are optimised offline and pushed per shift.

599
00:44:48.331 --> 00:44:49.803
Apps are offline-first.

600
00:44:50.083 --> 00:44:55.331
Planning drives cost: network design (MILP), sort plans, trip plans, load planning.

601
00:44:55.611 --> 00:44:57.147
Prediction drives service:

602
00:44:57.427 --> 00:44:59.262
TAT, ETA, at-risk shipments.

603
00:44:59.542 --> 00:45:07.030
India-specific depth: address intelligence, COD cash chains, NDR or RTO workflows, e-way bills.

604
00:45:08.380 --> 00:45:12.668
The data platform turns scans into control-tower visibility and ML.

605
00:45:12.948 --> 00:45:17.193
Supply chain planning extends the same data to shippers' inventory.

606
